# The transformer bodies the sealed view projects, compared between the two
# implementations.
#
# A body is what a transformer writes and what it reads: the messages its
# contract's rounds declare, together with the challenges those rounds
# sample. Kernel section 4 defines a central transformer as one with neither,
# so counting messages alone would track write-write interference and miss
# read-write interference, which is what BIND depends on.
#
# No judgment reads this. It is projected like the claim anchors and the
# statement labels beside it, for a consumer asking whether two transformers
# commute -- the rule class that composes two claims in parallel is the one
# that will, and it does not ship. Which is exactly why it is compared here:
# a projection nobody reads is a projection that drifts, and the two
# implementations have now disagreed three times about what a body is.
#
# The first artifact carries a transformer that owns no message at all. Two
# shipped contracts declare a message-free round, and under a message-only
# criterion such a reduction had an empty extent and left the question
# entirely.
#
# RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir -p %t
# RUN: zkc-seal %S/../Encoding/kzg-after.mlir %zkc-seal-full -o %t/batch.artifacts > /dev/null
# RUN: %python %S/../SemanticClosure/lib/artifact_handoff.py stage %t/batch.artifacts %t/batch.mlirbc
# RUN: zkc-derive --describe --signature=%zkc-registry-dir/soundness-signature.json \
# RUN:   --protocol-vocabulary=%zkc-registry-dir/protocol-vocabulary.json \
# RUN:   --construction-profile-registry=%zkc-registry-dir/construction-profiles.json \
# RUN:   %t/batch.mlirbc | grep "transformer body" > %t/batch.bodies
# RUN: FileCheck %s --check-prefix=BATCH < %t/batch.bodies
# BATCH: transformer body: batch events 5..5 non-central
# RUN: %if uv %{ %uv python -m oracle.parity bodies kzg-batch > %t/batch.oracle %}
# RUN: %if uv %{ diff %t/batch.bodies %t/batch.oracle %}

# Three transformers whose bodies are disjoint blocks, so the chain says what
# a body's extent is when nothing interleaves.
